## 4. State machines

### 4.1 Node status lifecycle (§4.2 `status`)

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> candidate: I-INGEST (x5 — everything lands candidate)

    candidate --> canonical: GUARD evidence >= 1 EvidenceRef\nAND confidence.method != ingest_prior\n(never a frequency threshold)
    candidate --> refuted: falsifier observed (K6)
    candidate --> superseded: replaced before promotion

    canonical --> refuted: falsifier observed
    canonical --> superseded: newer node `supersedes` it; valid_until closes

    refuted --> [*]: TERMINAL — node RETAINED (checkmark-1, FPL-2)
    superseded --> [*]: TERMINAL — node RETAINED (K4: no DELETE exists)

    note right of candidate
      Frequency of occurrence may raise
      REVIEW PRIORITY (facet sakg.review_priority)
      and never appears in confidence.method.
      It is not an edge on this diagram.
    end note

    note right of refuted
      No transition leaves refuted. A refuted
      decision is high-value knowledge; its
      replacement is a NEW node carrying a
      `supersedes` edge, not a status rewrite.
    end note
```

**Deliberate non-edges**, each a design decision rather than an omission:
- **No `deleted` state.** The alphabet has no such symbol (§2.2 `status` enum) and
  `unevaluatedProperties: false` forbids a tombstone field. Deletion is *unrepresentable*, which is
  the schema's share of INV-4; the store's append-only guarantee (K4, `test_store_append_only.py`)
  is the other share.
- **No `canonical → candidate` demotion.** §7 says nodes below `min_confidence` are *withheld*,
  never silently upranked — withholding is a **query-time filter**, not a status change. Adding a
  demotion edge would make status non-monotonic and break `as_of` reproducibility.
- **No `refuted → canonical` re-admission.** K6's re-admission rule ("evidence strictly exceeding
  the original scar") is satisfied by minting a new node, preserving the refutation record.

Each state's schema-checkable obligations:

| State | Schema obligation (Class A, §6) |
|---|---|
| `candidate` | envelope well-formed; `confidence.method` may be `ingest_prior` with empty `inputs` |
| `canonical` | `evidence` minItems 1; `confidence.method ∈ {evidence_binding, outcome_join, truth_maintenance_propagation, decay}` |
| `superseded` | `valid_until` non-null |
| `refuted` | `falsifier` a non-empty string |

### 4.2 Validation pipeline — which layer owns which invariant

This is the state machine that makes §6's honesty claim concrete: it shows exactly where the
schema stops and another owner begins.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Syntactic
    Syntactic: JSON parse\nOWNER: accept cmd 1
    Syntactic --> Structural: parses
    Syntactic --> Rejected: malformed

    Structural: envelope.schema.json\nClass A — types, enums, patterns,\nlengths, per-status conditionals\nOWNER: F-ENVELOPE (this node)
    Structural --> CrossField: valid
    Structural --> Rejected: ValidationError

    CrossField: id <-> namespace <-> type agreement;\nvalid_until >= valid_from\nClass B-1 — OWNER: test module T10 + I-STORE
    CrossField --> Transition: consistent
    CrossField --> Rejected: inconsistent

    Transition: legality of (prev_status -> new_status);\nappend-only, no overwrite\nClass B-2 — OWNER: I-STORE, I-INGEST
    Transition --> Graph: legal
    Transition --> Rejected: illegal

    Graph: confidence recompute propagation along\nderived_from / supports; acyclic dependency edges\nClass C — OWNER: I-STORE (S1 edges) + slice S3 (TMS)
    Graph --> Accepted: consistent
    Graph --> Rejected: inconsistent

    Accepted --> [*]
    Rejected --> [*]
```

Every state has an entry and an exit; every rejection path terminates. No invariant from §4.2 is
left without an owning stage — see the §6 matrix.

